        <div class="guide-label">PipelineSync field notes</div>
        <h2>What does duplicate data in HubSpot cost?</h2>
        <p class="guide-answer">Duplicate data costs you in four places: marketing sends to the wrong people or misses suppression rules, sales loses context and argues over ownership, service answers without the full history, and leadership stops trusting the reports. The record count is the visible part; lost time and lost confidence are the real cost.</p>
        <p class="intro">Duplicates are easy to dismiss as a database problem. In practice they split activity history, confuse ownership, weaken segmentation, and force people to spend time deciding which record to trust.</p>

        <section id="marketing">
          <h2>How do duplicates affect marketing?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Marketing feels duplicates first. The same person can receive the same email twice, or receive a campaign after unsubscribing on another record. Segments and lists overcount, so engagement rates look worse than they are, and attribution splits a buyer's journey across records so no single campaign gets credit.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Consent and unsubscribes stored on one record but not the other.</li>
            <li>Inflated list sizes and diluted engagement metrics.</li>
            <li>First-touch and last-touch credit split across records.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="sales">
          <h2>How do duplicates affect sales?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">For sales, duplicates mean lost context and routing mistakes. A rep opens one record and misses the calls and emails logged on the other, two reps contact the same buyer, and leads route to the wrong owner because ownership differs between copies. Every disconnected record adds a small decision tax.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Activity history split across records.</li>
            <li>Ownership conflicts and double outreach.</li>
            <li>Deals associated with the wrong company, so account totals are wrong.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="service">
          <h2>How do duplicates affect service and success?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Service teams answer tickets without the customer's full history when tickets attach to a duplicate contact or company. They miss open deals, recent complaints and contract details, which makes the customer repeat themselves and makes renewal and upsell conversations harder.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="reporting">
          <h2>How do duplicates affect reporting?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Reporting is where duplicates do the most lasting damage. Customer counts, conversion rates and revenue by account all drift when the same buyer exists more than once. Once leadership catches one wrong number, every report gets checked in a spreadsheet, and the CRM stops being the source of truth.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="patterns">
          <h2>What should you check before fixing records?</h2>
          <p class="guide-answer">Do not start by merging everything that looks similar. Segment suspected duplicates by source, object, owner, last activity and match confidence, so you understand why they were created and which fields should decide the primary record. Fixing the source matters more than fixing the records.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Compare email, domain, phone, external ID and normalised name.</li>
            <li>Identify the imports and integrations that create repeat records.</li>
            <li>Protect consent, lifecycle and ownership fields during merges.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>The full process is in our <a href="/resources/crm-cleanup-deduplication-system-pipelinesync/">HubSpot cleanup and deduplication system</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="checklist">
          <h2>What to change this week</h2>
          <div class="guide-checklist"><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Duplicate definition agreed</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Match confidence rules set</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Primary-record hierarchy documented</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Consent fields protected</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Creation sources identified</div></div><div class="guide-check"><span>✓</span><div>Prevention monitoring live</div></div></div>
